-- Where Do You Find Clients?
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Name the places VA work is actually found and what each costs you', 'Present yourself credibly with no client history', 'Spot the offers that are not worth answering'],
  notes = 'There are three routes, and they are not equally good for someone starting here.

GLOBAL FREELANCE PLATFORMS. Real work, real volume, and you are competing with the whole world on price with no reviews. They also take a cut. They work, slowly, if you specialise narrowly rather than offering everything. Do not make this your only route.

DIRECT APPROACH. Small businesses, consultants, coaches, agencies, online shops — anyone visibly busy and visibly disorganised. Slower to start, better paid, and nobody takes a percentage. This is where the good clients are.

REFERRAL. Eventually the main source, and Module 6 is about earning it.

WITH NO HISTORY, SHOW THE WORK INSTEAD. Nobody needs to have paid you for it to count. Build a sample: a properly validated spreadsheet, a written procedure, a tidy calendar, a sorted inbox structure. Put three of these somewhere shareable. ''Here is a client report I formatted'' beats any description of yourself.

BE SPECIFIC ABOUT WHO YOU HELP. ''I am a virtual assistant'' is forgettable. ''I do inbox and scheduling for coaches and consultants'' gets remembered and recommended, because people can only refer you for something they can name.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — protect yourself from the offers that waste or cost. Treat as warning signs: anyone asking you to pay a fee to start; a long unpaid ''test'' beyond a short sample; a client who will not say what the work actually is; and anyone wanting you to receive money and forward it on, which is a common fraud that leaves you carrying it. Also weigh the rate honestly — an hourly figure that looks large in dollars is smaller after the platform cut, the transfer cost and the hours you spend unpaid on messages. Work out what a job leaves you before you accept it.

And do not overlook the clients nearest you. Businesses in your own town also need inboxes managed, data entered and orders tracked, they pay in shillings with no transfer cost or time-zone difficulty, and you can meet them in person — which no overseas competitor can.',
  practice_activity = 'Write your one-line specific description: what you do, for whom. Then build three samples this week and put them somewhere you can send a link to. Finally, make two lists — five businesses you can approach directly and one platform to try — and contact three of the five before you touch the platform.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why is a specific description better than ''I am a virtual assistant''?", "options": ["People can only refer you for something they can name", "It sounds more experienced", "It allows you to charge more per hour", "Platforms rank specific profiles higher"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "Which offer should you refuse outright?", "options": ["Anyone asking you to receive money and forward it on", "A short paid trial task", "A client who wants a written agreement", "A client in a different time zone"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'a2126ba6-5c34-436b-a24c-d2faf970d851';

-- Should You Work For Free?
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Tell the difference between a sample and being used', 'Set a limit on unpaid work before you are asked for any', 'Ask for the things that make unpaid work worth doing'],
  notes = 'The honest answer is: rarely, briefly, and on your terms.

WHEN UNPAID WORK IS REASONABLE. A short test task — an hour at most — that a client pays attention to and that genuinely shows whether you can do the job. Something you make for your own portfolio, unasked, because you own it. A small favour for someone who has actually helped you.

WHEN IT IS NOT. A ''test'' that is really a live job with a deadline and a deliverable. Anything open-ended. Work promised to lead to paid work later, where later is never defined. And the classic: exposure, a testimonial, or ''lots more coming'' offered instead of money.

THE TEST THAT SETTLES IT: would this task have to be done by someone if you did not do it? If yes, it is work, and work is paid. A sample is something made to demonstrate capability. A job is something the business needed done.

SET THE LIMIT BEFORE YOU ARE ASKED, because in the moment, with a first client in front of you, you will say yes. One hour, once, per prospective client. Write it down now.

IF YOU DO WORK UNPAID, get something for it in writing: permission to show the work, a testimonial you can quote, or a named introduction to someone else. Ask on the day you deliver, while they are pleased.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — there is a pattern to watch for, and it is common where the client is abroad and the worker is not. The unpaid trial that grows; the first month''s payment that keeps being delayed; the rate that was agreed and then revised downward once you have started. The protection is unglamorous and it works: agree the scope and the rate in writing before starting, take a deposit or invoice the first period up front, and deliver a small piece and get paid for it before committing to a large one. A client who will not agree to a first small paid piece has told you what you need to know, and walking away costs you nothing but a week.',
  practice_activity = 'Write your unpaid-work rule in one sentence and keep it where you will see it when someone asks. Then write the message you will send when a request crosses it — polite, brief, offering the paid alternative. Having the words ready is what makes you able to say them.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What test distinguishes a sample from unpaid work?", "options": ["Whether someone would have had to do the task anyway — if so, it is work and work is paid", "How long it takes", "Whether the client is friendly", "Whether it is for a large company"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What protects you from the trial that never converts to payment?", "options": ["Scope and rate in writing first, then a small paid piece before committing to a large one", "Working faster to prove yourself", "Accepting a lower rate to secure the client", "Doing more free work to build trust"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '06ec02e8-80d9-47b9-b22f-d27b95d5ef8d';

-- 4 Tips On How To Handle Multiple Clients As A Virtual Assistant
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Run several clients without any of them feeling like a second priority', 'Decide what to do first when two clients want the same hour', 'Recognise when you are full before the work suffers'],
  notes = 'More clients is the goal and also where the work starts going wrong, because the failure is quiet: nothing breaks, everything just becomes slightly late.

SEPARATE EVERYTHING. A distinct folder, board and list per client. Never a shared file with two clients'' data in it — that is both a mistake waiting to happen and a confidentiality breach if it ever leaves your machine.

BLOCK TIME PER CLIENT rather than switching whenever a message arrives. Two focused hours on one client beats four hours shared between three, and switching between people costs you more than you think. Tell each client roughly when their work happens, so silence in between is expected rather than worrying.

SET RESPONSE EXPECTATIONS EXPLICITLY. ''I reply within the working day'' is a promise you can keep. Instant replies at all hours is not, and it is the habit that turns into resentment and then burnout. Say it once at the start and it becomes normal.

WHEN TWO CLIENTS WANT THE SAME HOUR: whichever is genuinely more urgent goes first, and you TELL THE OTHER ONE IMMEDIATELY with a new time. Clients rarely mind waiting. They mind not being told. Silence is what loses the contract, not delay.

KNOW YOUR CAPACITY, in real hours, allowing for the work you do not bill — messages, invoicing, finding the next client, and your own life. The warning signs that you are past it: working late routinely, missing small things, dreading a particular client''s name. At that point you either raise your rates and take fewer clients, or you stop accepting work. Both are better than the third option, which is failing slowly in front of everyone.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — keep a simple record per client of what you did and when, updated as you go. It takes a minute, it makes invoicing honest and quick, it answers ''what have you actually been doing'' with specifics, and it is the evidence behind every rate rise you will ever ask for.',
  practice_activity = 'Map one full week honestly: every hour you actually have, blocks for each client, and time for the unbilled work. Be truthful about sleep, family and travel. Look at what remains — that is your real capacity, and it is almost always less than you assumed. Then write your response-time promise in one sentence and use it with your next client.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Two clients need the same hour. What matters most?", "options": ["Telling the one who waits immediately, with a new time — clients mind silence more than delay", "Working faster so both are done", "Choosing the client who pays more", "Splitting the hour between them"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What are the signs you have taken on too much?", "options": ["Routinely working late, missing small things, dreading a client''s name", "Having to use a task board", "Being asked questions by clients", "Having more than one client"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = '6f8abebc-aa61-4166-a253-83878d9fd072';

-- How Do Virtual Assistants Track Client Hours Accurately?
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Record time in a way both you and the client can trust', 'Invoice clearly enough that nobody queries it', 'Decide when hourly billing should become a fixed fee'],
  notes = 'If you bill by time, the record is the product. A number you produced from memory at the end of the month is not one either of you can defend.

TRACK AS YOU GO, never afterwards. Reconstructed hours are always wrong, and they are wrong in your disfavour, because the work you forget is work you do not get paid for.

RECORD WHAT, NOT JUST HOW LONG. ''2.5 hrs — inbox triage and drafted 12 replies'' is a line nobody queries. ''2.5 hrs — admin'' invites a question you will spend twenty minutes answering.

COUNT THE INVISIBLE WORK, and agree at the start that it counts: reading a brief, client calls, the back-and-forth of messages, learning their system. All of it is time you are not selling to anyone else. Beginners bill only the obvious task and quietly work half again as long for nothing.

ROUND HONESTLY and consistently, to five or fifteen minutes, in whichever direction your rule says — the same rule every time.

INVOICE PREDICTABLY: the same day each month, itemised, with your payment details and how long they have to pay. A clear invoice is paid faster, and being the person whose invoices are easy to process is worth more than most people realise.

WHEN TO STOP BILLING HOURLY. Once you know a recurring job well, hourly pricing starts punishing you: you become faster, so you earn less for the same result. That is the moment to move to a fixed monthly fee for a defined set of work. You get predictable income, the client gets a predictable cost, and every efficiency you find is yours.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — chase unpaid invoices calmly and early. One polite reminder the day after it is due, a second a week later, and then stop work until it is settled, having said in advance that you would. This is normal professional practice, not aggression. And keep your own records: what you invoiced, what arrived, and what the transfer cost you. Getting money across a border is not free, and the figure that reaches you is the only one that matters.',
  practice_activity = 'Track every working minute for one week, writing what you did rather than only how long. At the end, compare the total against what you would have guessed. Then produce one real itemised invoice from it, including a line for the invisible work, and read it as if you were the client — would you query anything?',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "Why track time as you go rather than at the end of the month?", "options": ["Reconstructed hours are always wrong, and wrong in your disfavour", "It is required by clients", "It looks more professional", "It makes the invoice longer"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "When should hourly billing become a fixed monthly fee?", "options": ["Once you know a recurring job well, since getting faster then earns you less", "As soon as a client asks", "After the first invoice", "Only for very large clients"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'cece19cf-8737-4269-ac3d-1d15d48064ee';

-- How to Raise Your Virtual Assistant Rates
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Judge when a rate rise is justified and when it is not', 'Tell an existing client without apologising for it', 'Raise what you earn without only raising the number'],
  notes = 'Most VAs start too low, stay there too long, and then ask apologetically. All three are fixable.

WHEN IT IS JUSTIFIED: you are demonstrably faster or better than when you started; you have taken on more responsibility than was agreed; you are fully booked and turning work away; or your costs have risen. Being fully booked is the clearest signal there is — if nobody ever declines your price, it is too low.

BUILD THE EVIDENCE FIRST. This is where the record from the last lesson earns its keep: what you have delivered, what you have taken on beyond the original scope, what problems you caught. You are not asking for a favour, you are repricing a service that has changed.

HOW TO SAY IT. Give notice — a month is normal. Say it plainly and once: ''From the first of next month my rate for this work will be X.'' No apology, no lengthy justification, no listing your personal costs. Over-explaining invites negotiation; a plain statement usually receives a plain acceptance.

EXPECT TO LOSE ONE OCCASIONALLY, and count it correctly. A client who leaves over a modest rise was usually the one taking the most time for the least money. Their hours are now available for someone paying properly.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the number is not the only lever, and often not the best one. You also earn more by moving from hourly to a fixed monthly fee for a defined scope; by taking on the work that pays better than general admin, such as bookkeeping support or inbox ownership; by charging for a system you built rather than the hours you spent building it; and by pricing a client abroad in their currency where that is what their market pays, rather than converting your local rate downward. Raise new clients first — test the higher number on people with no history of paying you less, and you will find it accepted more easily than you expect.',
  practice_activity = 'Work out what you actually earn per hour right now, after unbilled work and transfer costs — the real figure, not the quoted one. Then write your rate-rise message in three sentences, with no apology and no justification beyond the notice period. Send it to your next new enquiry at the higher number before you send it to anyone existing.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "What is the clearest signal that your rate is too low?", "options": ["You are fully booked and nobody ever declines your price", "Other VAs charge more", "You have been working for a year", "A client asks for a discount"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "How should a rate rise be communicated?", "options": ["Plainly, once, with notice and no apology or lengthy justification", "With a detailed explanation of your rising costs", "As an apology with an offer to negotiate", "Without notice, on the next invoice"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'f3e54e18-1019-4d26-a466-9f2835eee6fa';

-- Become a Referral Magnet As a Virtual Assistant
update public.lessons set
  learning_objectives = array['Make your work easy to recommend', 'Ask for a referral at the moment it will be given', 'Build income that does not depend on constantly looking for work'],
  notes = 'Referral is how established VAs get almost all their work, and it is not luck. It is a small number of deliberate habits.

BE EASY TO RECOMMEND. A person recommends you when they can say what you do in one sentence and are confident you will not embarrass them. That means being specific about your service, and it means never letting a referred client down — a bad referral costs the person who made it, and they will not make another.

ASK, AND ASK AT THE RIGHT MOMENT. Most people never ask, and clients rarely think of it unprompted. The moment is the day you deliver something they are visibly pleased with: ''I am glad that worked. If you know anyone who needs the same, I would appreciate an introduction.'' One sentence, no awkwardness, extremely effective.

MAKE IT EASY. Give them something to forward — a line describing what you do and a link to your samples. A referral that requires the client to compose a paragraph often does not happen.

REFER OTHERS. When a job is outside your skills or your capacity, send it to someone who can do it. It comes back, and in the meantime you have become someone who is worth knowing rather than only someone looking for work.

STAY IN CONTACT AFTER THE WORK ENDS. A short message twice a year to a past client costs nothing. People move companies and take their suppliers with them.

ADDITION BEYOND THE VIDEO — the goal underneath all of this is to stop looking for work. That happens when two things are true: most clients are on a fixed monthly arrangement rather than one-off tasks, and new ones arrive by introduction. Then your time goes into doing the work and improving it instead of hunting for the next job, which is the difference between freelancing and having a business. Aim for it deliberately from the first client, not after the twentieth. And in a market where much of this runs on personal networks — a church, a sacco, an old classmate, a chama — the local reputation you build is not a lesser version of an online profile. It is usually the stronger one.',
  practice_activity = 'Write your one-sentence description and a short forwardable message about what you do, with a link to your samples. Then ask three people — past clients, or anyone who has seen your work — for an introduction, using the words from this lesson. Ask on a day something has just gone well, not on a day you are short of work.',
  knowledge_check = '[{"question": "When is the moment to ask for a referral?", "options": ["The day you deliver something the client is visibly pleased with", "When you are short of work", "At the end of the contract", "In your first week, to set expectations"], "correct_index": 0}, {"question": "What does it take to stop constantly looking for work?", "options": ["Most clients on fixed monthly arrangements, and new ones arriving by introduction", "A larger number of one-off clients", "A profile on more platforms", "Charging the lowest rate in the market"], "correct_index": 0}]'::jsonb
where id = 'dbd3c502-60f9-4585-a7bf-8c0bb8d406c8';
